# The tense rule catches a change described from the past; this one
# catches a claim dated to the present or promised for later. Bare
# `currently` is too common as runtime state (`the currently selected
# row`, `whether the item is currently available`), so it counts only
# before a word that names support or a limit, or after a negated or
# modal auxiliary. `TODO` stays out, because a code comment uses it
# as a marker.
#
# Three phrases keep a runtime sense behind a guard. `in the future`
# skips a form of `be` or a time noun before it (`the timestamp must
# be in the future`, `a cutoff in the future`); the filter narrows the
# finding to the phrase itself, and a glued comma has its own branch
# so the sentence opener (`In the future, ...`) still counts once.
# `is planned` skips an agent (`the query is planned by the
# optimizer`), with a clause-end twin because a negated word after a
# space needs a word to consume. `to be implemented` needs `yet`,
# `still`, or `remains` before it, since the bare infinitive names a
# capability (`allows a filesystem to be implemented in user space`).
expr when [be, is, are, was, were, time, date, point, timestamp, deadline, cutoff, moment, instant, far, further]
expr main [(currently [supports, support, only, unsupported, not, no, limited, lacks, lack, requires, require, returns, accepts, uses, defaults, ignores, handles, allows, disallows, broken, missing, unimplemented, experimental, deprecated]), ([does, doesn't, do, don't, cannot, can't, will, won't] currently), (not currently), (at the moment), (right now), (for now), (at present), (as of now), (at this time), (as of today), (at the time of writing), (as of this writing), (in the current version), (in the current release), (in the latest version), (in the latest release), (in this version), (in this release), (going forward), <(!@when in the future!,), (in the future)>, (in the future,), (in a future release), (in a future version), (in an upcoming release), (in upcoming releases), (in future releases), (in future versions), (will be added), (will be supported), (will be removed), (will be deprecated), ([is, are] planned !by), ([is, are] planned!( )), (on the roadmap), (coming soon), (not yet implemented), (not yet supported), ([yet, still, remains, remain] to be implemented)]
let message "Delta tense. Durable prose describes the system as it is, and git history records what changed."
let description "Flags phrases that date a claim to the current version or promise one for a later one."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Available reports whether the item is currently available."
allows "Cursor returns the currently selected row."
allows "The parser supports tables."
allows "TODO: handle the empty case."
allows "The timestamp must be in the future."
allows "A since cutoff in the future excludes the row."
allows "The query is planned by the optimizer."
allows "The library allows a filesystem to be implemented in user space."
